    43	**  
    44	Bariatric surgery in Perth, with a specialist team that gets it**</span>
    45	
    46	<span class="mark">If you are living with overweight or obesity and feel
    47	like you have tried everything to lose weight, you are not alone in
    48	considering bariatric surgery as an alternative. Obesity Surgery WA has
    49	been a trusted name in Perth weight loss for over 20 years and offers
    50	gastric sleeve surgery, gastric bypass, mini bypass, gastric band, and
    51	revision surgery at Mount Private Hospital, The Park Private Hospital,
    52	and Waikiki Private Hospital. Our dedicated patient care team also
    53	offers GLP-1 medical weight management, dietetic support, and exercise
    54	physiology in-clinic at Booragoon and Greenfields.</span>
    55	
    56	### **<span class="mark">What is bariatric surgery?</span>**
    57	
    58	<span class="mark">  
    59	Bariatric surgery is a group of surgical procedures that change how the
    60	stomach (and sometimes small intestine) processes and responds to food.
    61	The goal of bariatric surgery is earlier satiety (the feeling of being
    62	full), reduced hunger, and improved metabolic health, resulting in
    63	weight loss. It is a medical intervention for obesity when diet and
    64	exercise have not been enough.</span>
    65	
    66	<span class="mark">At Obesity Surgery WA, we recognise that obesity is a
    67	chronic medical condition, not a willpower problem. Likewise, we don’t
    68	view bariatric surgery as a quick fix or a solution that works in
    69	isolation from the rest of your health and wellbeing goals. Surgery
    70	treats the medical condition; long-term results are shaped by the
    71	quality and consistency of the support and habits we build around
    72	it.</span>
    73	
    74	<span class="mark">Most bariatric surgeries in Australia are performed
    75	as laparoscopic keyhole surgeries, meaning less pain post-operatively,
    76	smaller scars, and generally, a faster recovery than open surgery. No
    77	procedure is a ‘one-size-fits-all’; your surgeon will spend time
    78	understanding your weight loss goals and recommend a procedure based on
    79	those, any accompanying health conditions, and your lifestyle.</span>
    80	
    81	- <span class="mark">Closing comparison note (below paragraphs): Orient
    82	  the reader to what they will find next — a plain overview of the four
    83	  main surgical options so they can walk into their consultation with a
    84	  sense of what the conversation will
    85	  cover.</span><img src="media/image1.png" style="width:6.26772in;height:1.16667in" />
    86	
    87	### 
    88	
    89	### **<span class="mark">Am I eligible for bariatric surgery? </span>**
    90	
    91	<span class="mark">Bariatric surgery is generally considered suitable
    92	for adults with a BMI of 40 or higher, or in specific circumstances for
    93	those with a BMI of 35 or higher when an obesity-related health
    94	condition is also present. People with a BMI between 30 and 35 may be
    95	candidates in specific circumstances, particularly where type 2 diabetes
    96	is poorly controlled.</span>
    97	
    98	**<span class="mark">Eligibility for bariatric surgery generally
    99	includes:</span>**
   100	
   101	- <span class="mark">BMI 40+: surgery is generally an appropriate option
   102	  to discuss, regardless of other conditions</span>
   103	
   104	- <span class="mark">BMI 35–40 and a related condition such as type 2
   105	  diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, joint disease, or
   106	  gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD): surgery is commonly
   107	  considered</span>
   108	
   109	- <span class="mark">BMI 30–35: may be considered in select cases, often
   110	  where diabetes is difficult to manage with medication alone; assessed
   111	  on an individual basis</span>
   112	
   113	- <span class="mark">History of genuine attempts at weight loss through
   114	  diet, exercise, or medical means without lasting success</span>
   115	
   116	- <span class="mark">Commitment to long-term dietary and lifestyle
   117	  changes, supplements where needed, and ongoing follow-up:
   118	  understanding that surgery is your starting point, and aftercare is
   119	  what protects your results</span>
   120	
   121	- <span class="mark">Final eligibility is always determined after a
   122	  comprehensive assessment with your surgeon and (as needed) Obesity
   123	  Surgery WA’s specialist care team, including medical history review
   124	  and review of any conditions that affect surgical risk</span>
   125	
   126	### **<span class="mark">Bariatric surgery: how it works</span>**
   127	
   128	### <span class="mark">**Initial consultation and assessment** Here you’ll meet your surgeon, talk through your medical history and weight loss goals, ask as many questions as you like, and receive a professional opinion about which procedure (gastric sleeve, bypass, band, or medical weight loss treatment pathways) is most suitable for you and your lifestyle. </span>
   129	
   130	2.  <span class="mark">**Assessment with the wider team**  
   131	    Once you have decided which procedure or pathway is the right one
   132	    for you, you’ll meet with members of our</span>
   133	    <span class="mark">patient care team as needed, including in-house
   134	    physician, dietitians, and exercise physiologist, to help you
   135	    prepare for the changes ahead and plan your pre-op dietary needs.  
   136	    </span>
   137	
   138	3.  <span class="mark">**Pre-surgery preparation**  
   139	    Your surgeon will explain your procedure and individual recovery
   140	    plan, and you may need to follow a specific pre-op diet to shrink
   141	    your liver and reduce surgical risk (typically 2–4 weeks). But you
   142	    won’t do it alone! Our staff will support you throughout.  
   143	    </span>
   144	
   145	4.  <span class="mark">**Procedure day  **
   146	    Obesity Surgery WA’s bariatric surgery is performed via keyhole
   147	    surgery in most cases, with short post-operative hospital stays (1-3
   148	    nights) at [<u>Mount Private
   149	    Hospital</u>](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [<u>The Park
   150	    Private Hospital</u>](https://www.tpph.com.au/), or [<u>Waikiki
   151	    Private Hospital</u>](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/) in Perth.
   152	    Guided by nursing and dietetic staff, you’ll start on fluids and
   153	    your recovery will be monitored until you’re ready to return home.  
   154	    </span>
   155	
   156	5.  <span class="mark">**Long-term aftercare  **
   157	    Most light daily activities can usually be resumed gradually from
   158	    around the ~2-3 week mark, with return to work depending on which
   159	    procedure you have and what type of work you do (~2+ weeks’ for desk
   160	    work/4+ weeks for physical or manual labour). Your post-op
   161	    progression from fluids only to regular textures will take place
   162	    with the support of your dietitian. Plus you’ll need ongoing reviews
   163	    (regularly post-op then annually) and bloodwork done to protect your
   164	    nutrition and results long-term.</span>
   165	
   166	### **<span class="mark"> Common bariatric procedures, and what to expect from each </span>**
   167	
   168	- <span class="mark">**Gastric sleeve (sleeve gastrectomy)**: our most
   169	  common procedure and suitable for a wide range of patients. A large
   170	  portion of the stomach is removed, leaving a slim tube (the ‘sleeve’)
   171	  through which food moves much more slowly, and reduces ghrelin
   172	  (‘hunger hormone’). Many people lose ~60–70% of excess weight, though
   173	  individual results will vary.  
   174	  </span>
   175	
   176	- <span class="mark">**Gastric bypass (Roux-en-Y)**: restricts food
   177	  intake and changes how nutrients are absorbed. Typically results in
   178	  greater weight loss than a sleeve, often ~70–80% of excess weight,
   179	  though individual results will vary. Frequently preferred for type 2
   180	  diabetes due to its effect on blood sugar. Requires lifelong vitamin
   181	  and mineral monitoring and supplementation.  
   182	  </span>
   183	
   184	- <span class="mark">**Mini gastric bypass (one anastomosis bypass)**: a
   185	  simplified variation of the Roux-en-Y bypass that creates a single
   186	  connection between the stomach and small intestine, rather than two.
   187	  The result is a shorter, less complex operation with a similar weight
   188	  loss outcome for most patients, though with a slightly different risk
   189	  profile. Your surgeon can advise whether it's a suitable option for
   190	  your circumstances.  
   191	  </span>
   192	
   193	- <span class="mark">**Gastric band (adjustable band)**: a reversible
   194	  procedure in which an inflatable silicone band is placed around the
   195	  upper stomach, creating a small pouch that slows eating and promotes
   196	  fullness. No cutting or stapling is involved and the band can be
   197	  adjusted or removed. It generally produces the least weight loss of
   198	  the surgical options and requires ongoing follow-up for adjustments.  
   199	  </span>
   200	
   201	- <span class="mark">**Revision surgery**: for patients who have had a
   202	  previous bariatric procedure and require a conversion, a repair, or
   203	  further intervention (for example, a band that is no longer working
   204	  well or causing complications). Revision surgery is generally more
   205	  complex than a primary procedure, and risks and benefits will be
   206	  assessed individually with your surgeon.  
   207	  </span>
   208	
   209	- <span class="mark">**Medical weight management**: non-surgical weight
   210	  loss treatment pathways including GLP-1 medications (such as
   211	  semaglutide) and structured dietitian-led programs. Suits people who
   212	  do not want surgery, are not yet candidates, or who would like
   213	  specialist medical support before or instead of proceeding with a
   214	  surgical procedure.</span>
   215	
   216	### **<span class="mark">Bariatric surgery risks, complications, and side effects </span>**
   217	
   218	<span class="mark">Every surgical procedure carries risk, though modern
   219	keyhole techniques and highly experienced surgeons make bariatric
   220	procedures safe for most suitable candidates. Below are some of the most
   221	common risks, potential complications, and side effects of undergoing
   222	bariatric surgery:</span>
   223	
   224	- <span class="mark">General surgical risks include bleeding, infection,
   225	  blood clots, and adverse reactions to anaesthetic — these apply to any
   226	  surgical procedure.</span>
   227	
   228	- <span class="mark">Procedure-specific risks include leaks at staple or
   229	  join lines (sleeve and bypass) and band slippage or erosion (gastric
   230	  band).</span>
   231	
   232	- <span class="mark">Nutritional changes are common, particularly with
   233	  bypass procedures, which affect vitamin and mineral absorption and
   234	  require lifelong supplementation and monitoring.</span>
   235	
   236	- <span class="mark">Eating habits will change — smaller portions,
   237	  slower eating, and in some cases avoiding certain foods, particularly
   238	  in the early months following surgery.</span>
   239	
   240	- <span class="mark">Weight regain is possible if lifestyle habits
   241	  aren't sustained; surgery is a powerful tool but works best alongside
   242	  ongoing dietary and behavioural support.</span>
   243	
   244	- <span class="mark">Revision surgery may be required in some cases,
   245	  depending on the procedure and how an individual responds over
   246	  time.</span>
   247	
   248	### **<span class="mark">Understanding the costs of bariatric surgery</span>** 
   249	
   250	<span class="mark">The total cost of bariatric surgery varies depending
   251	on the procedure, your level of private health cover, your hospital
   252	excess, and anaesthetist fees. For patients with appropriate private
   253	health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) a
   254	significant portion of the overall cost is covered. Medicare rebates
   255	apply when eligibility criteria are met, and for some patients, early
   256	access to superannuation is available under specific circumstances.
   257	Before any commitment is made, our team provides a clear, itemised
   258	breakdown of what applies to your situation — no surprises, no pressure,
   259	just the information you need to plan with confidence.</span>
   260	
   261	### **<span class="mark">Conversion CTA headline</span>**
   262	
   263	<span class="mark">  
   264	You've done the hard work getting here. Let's talk about what comes
   265	next.  
   266	Alt  
   267	Tired of wondering whether life could be different? Let’s talk.  
   268	</span>
   269	
   270	### <span class="mark">FAQs (7 questions) </span>
   271	
   272	<span class="mark">**1. How do I know which bariatric procedure is right
   273	for me?  **
   274	The right procedure depends on your health history, weight loss goals,
   275	existing health conditions (like reflux or diabetes), and your
   276	lifestyle. There is no universal answer; what works well for one person
   277	may not be the best fit for another. This is exactly what your initial
   278	consultation is for. Your surgeon will review your circumstances
   279	thoroughly and recommend the procedure most likely to deliver safe,
   280	sustained results for you specifically.</span>
   281	
   282	<span class="mark">**2. Is bariatric surgery available on Medicare or
   283	private health insurance?  **
   284	Medicare rebates apply to most bariatric procedures when eligibility
   285	criteria are met, and private health insurance with a gold-tier or
   286	equivalent policy covers the hospital and theatre component. The
   287	combination of both typically means a significant portion of the total
   288	cost is covered. Out-of-pocket costs vary depending on your level of
   289	cover, excess, and anaesthetist fees. Our team provides a clear,
   290	itemised cost breakdown before any commitment is made, so you know
   291	exactly where you stand.</span>
   292	
   293	<span class="mark">**3. What is the difference between a gastric sleeve
   294	and a gastric bypass?  **
   295	A sleeve gastrectomy reduces the size of the stomach, leaving digestion
   296	otherwise unchanged. A gastric bypass does the same but also reroutes
   297	the small intestine, meaning the body absorbs fewer kilojoules from
   298	food. The bypass tends to produce slightly greater weight loss and
   299	stronger outcomes for type 2 diabetes and reflux, but is a more complex
   300	procedure with greater long-term nutritional demands. Your surgeon will
   301	help determine which is the better procedure for your situation.</span>
   302	
   303	<span class="mark">**4. Do I have to have surgery, or are there
   304	non-surgical options?  **
   305	Surgery is not the only path. Medical weight management, including GLP-1
   306	medications and dietitian-led programs, is a genuine and effective
   307	option for many people, and our team offers this alongside surgical
   308	pathways. Some patients pursue medical management first and decide to
   309	progress to surgery later; others find it sufficient on its own. The
   310	goal is the right outcome for you, not a particular procedure.</span>
   311	
   312	<span class="mark">**5. What does aftercare involve, and why does it
   313	matter so much?  **
   314	Aftercare typically includes follow-up appointments with your surgeon,
   315	ongoing dietitian and exercise physiology support, nutritional
   316	monitoring via blood tests, and guidance on building sustainable habits.
   317	It matters because surgery changes your body, but long-term results are
   318	shaped by what happens in the months and years that follow. Patients who
   319	stay engaged with their care team consistently tend to achieve better
   320	long-term outcomes. At Obesity Surgery WA, we view your surgery as the
   321	beginning of your weight loss journey, not the end.</span>
   322	
   323	<span class="mark">**6. Where will my surgery and appointments take
   324	place?  **
