 Summary: Fix the location mismatch, missing stats bar, missing GP-referral step, current Google People-Also-Ask gaps and internal-link anchors before implementation.
+
+## Batch-1 gap-closing additions (G1-G5)
+
+### G1 — residual compliance claims
+
+1. **Laparoscopic recovery comparison**
+
+> Most bariatric surgeries in Australia are performed as laparoscopic keyhole surgeries, meaning less pain post-operatively, smaller scars, and generally, a faster recovery than open surgery. No procedure is a ‘one-size-fits-all’; your surgeon will spend time understanding your weight loss goals and recommend a procedure based on those, any accompanying health conditions, and your lifestyle.
+
+> Most bariatric surgeries are performed laparoscopically, using small incisions. Your surgeon will explain the technique, expected recovery, alternatives and individual risks, then discuss which procedure may be clinically appropriate for you.
+
+Why it matters: Remove the comparative pain and recovery claim; the remaining explanation is evidence-neutral and does not imply a better outcome.
+
+2. **Gastric bypass comparison and preference claim**
+
+> Gastric bypass (Roux-en-Y): restricts food intake and changes how nutrients are absorbed. Typically results in greater weight loss than a sleeve, often ~70–80% of excess weight, though individual results will vary. Frequently preferred for type 2 diabetes due to its effect on blood sugar. Requires lifelong vitamin and mineral monitoring and supplementation.
+
+> Gastric bypass (Roux-en-Y): changes stomach size and how food passes through the small intestine. It has procedure-specific nutritional requirements and risks; your surgeon can explain why it may or may not be appropriate for your circumstances.
+
+Why it matters: Remove the comparative weight-loss and “frequently preferred” claims from this hub summary.
+
+3. **Mini bypass comparative complexity and outcome claim**
+
+> Mini gastric bypass (one anastomosis bypass): a simplified variation of the Roux-en-Y bypass that creates a single connection between the stomach and small intestine, rather than two. The result is a shorter, less complex operation with a similar weight loss outcome for most patients, though with a slightly different risk profile. Your surgeon can advise whether it's a suitable option for your circumstances.
+
+> Mini gastric bypass (one anastomosis bypass): a bypass variation that creates a single connection between the stomach and small intestine. The surgeon can explain its technical features, risks, follow-up needs and whether it is appropriate for your circumstances.
+
+Why it matters: The draft makes comparative complexity and outcome claims that are unnecessary on a hub page.
+
+4. **Gastric-band comparative outcome claim**
+
+> Gastric band (adjustable band): a reversible procedure in which an inflatable silicone band is placed around the upper stomach, creating a small pouch that slows eating and promotes fullness. No cutting or stapling is involved and the band can be adjusted or removed. It generally produces the least weight loss of the surgical options and requires ongoing follow-up for adjustments.
+
+> Gastric band (adjustable band): an implanted adjustable band that can be adjusted or removed, but still requires surgery, ongoing reviews and monitoring for band-related complications. Your surgeon can explain the expected follow-up and whether it is appropriate for you.
+
+Why it matters: Remove the comparative “least weight loss” statement and avoid presenting reversibility as a low-risk shortcut.
+
+5. **Gastric-sleeve suitability and quantified outcome wording**
+
+> Gastric sleeve (sleeve gastrectomy): our most common procedure and suitable for a wide range of patients. A large portion of the stomach is removed, leaving a slim tube (the ‘sleeve’) through which food moves much more slowly, and reduces ghrelin (‘hunger hormone’). Many people lose ~60–70% of excess weight, though individual results will vary.
+
+> Gastric sleeve (sleeve gastrectomy): removes part of the stomach to create a smaller stomach. The surgeon will explain the expected effects, risks, nutritional needs and whether it is appropriate for you; individual results vary.
+
+Why it matters: Avoid broad suitability wording on the hub and leave procedure-specific outcome discussion to an assessed consultation and the child page.
+
+6. **Residual weight-loss assurance in the risks section**
+
+> Weight regain is possible if lifestyle habits aren't sustained; surgery is a powerful tool but works best alongside ongoing dietary and behavioural support.
+
+> Weight regain remains possible, and follow-up needs vary. Ongoing dietary and behavioural support can help the care team monitor your progress and adjust advice.
+
+Why it matters: Keep the risk disclosure without describing surgery as a “powerful tool” or implying a preferred treatment effect.
+
+7. **Residual comparative sleeve/bypass claim in FAQ 3**
+
+> A sleeve gastrectomy reduces the size of the stomach, leaving digestion otherwise unchanged. A gastric bypass does the same but also reroutes the small intestine, meaning the body absorbs fewer kilojoules from food. The bypass tends to produce slightly greater weight loss and stronger outcomes for type 2 diabetes and reflux, but is a more complex procedure with greater long-term nutritional demands. Your surgeon will help determine which is the better procedure for your situation.
+
+> A sleeve gastrectomy reduces the size of the stomach without rerouting the small intestine. A Roux-en-Y gastric bypass creates a smaller stomach pouch and reroutes part of the small intestine. Each has different risks, nutritional monitoring and follow-up needs; your surgeon can explain the differences for your circumstances.
+
+Why it matters: Remove comparative efficacy, reflux and “better procedure” language from the FAQ.
+
+8. **Duplicate Medicare/private-health coverage claim in FAQ 2**
+
+> Medicare rebates apply to most bariatric procedures when eligibility criteria are met, and private health insurance with a gold-tier or equivalent policy covers the hospital and theatre component. The combination of both typically means a significant portion of the total cost is covered. Out-of-pocket costs vary depending on your level of cover, excess, and anaesthetist fees. Our team provides a clear, itemised cost breakdown before any commitment is made, so you know exactly where you stand.
+
+> Medicare benefits and private-health benefits may apply when the relevant MBS, clinical, referral and policy requirements are met. Coverage, waiting periods, exclusions, excesses and out-of-pocket costs vary, so ask Medicare and your insurer to confirm what applies and discuss an itemised estimate at consultation.
+
+Why it matters: This is a second coverage claim after the cost paragraph; it must not promise that a gold-tier policy covers a component or that a significant portion will be covered.
+
+9. **Secondary GLP-1 references already covered by the first-pass recommendations**
+
+> Medical weight management: non-surgical weight loss treatment pathways including GLP-1 medications (such as semaglutide) and structured dietitian-led programs. Suits people who do not want surgery, are not yet candidates, or who would like specialist medical support before or instead of proceeding with a surgical procedure.
+
+> Medical weight management: a non-surgical pathway supported by medical assessment, dietitian input and long-term follow-up. Suitability should be assessed by the clinical team.
+
+> Surgery is not the only path. Medical weight management, including GLP-1 medications and dietitian-led programs, is a genuine and effective option for many people, and our team offers this alongside surgical pathways. Some patients pursue medical management first and decide to progress to surgery later; others find it sufficient on its own. The goal is the right outcome for you, not a particular procedure.
+
+> Surgery is not the only pathway. Some people may be better suited to a non-surgical medical weight management plan, dietitian-led support, or a staged approach before considering surgery; suitability should be assessed by the clinical team.
+
+Why it matters: These occurrences are covered by the earlier recommendations. Confirm the pending TGA/legal policy before retaining any medication wording; if retained, use active-ingredient/class names only (never brands).
+
+### G2 — MBS-inaccurate BMI wording
+
+1. **Eligibility summary**
+
+> Bariatric surgery is generally considered suitable for adults with a BMI of 40 or higher, or in specific circumstances for those with a BMI of 35 or higher when an obesity-related health condition is also present. People with a BMI between 30 and 35 may be candidates in specific circumstances, particularly where type 2 diabetes is poorly controlled.
+
+> MBS eligibility is assessed individually. The relevant thresholds are BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with a major obesity-related comorbidity. A BMI below 35 does not meet the MBS threshold; discuss any other clinical pathway with your surgeon.
+
+2. **BMI 40+**
+
+> BMI 40+: surgery is generally an appropriate option to discuss, regardless of other conditions
+
+> BMI 40+: MBS eligibility is assessed individually against the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements; BMI alone does not guarantee eligibility.
+
+3. **BMI 35-40**
+
+> BMI 35–40 and a related condition such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, joint disease, or gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD): surgery is commonly considered
+
+> BMI 35+: MBS eligibility is assessed individually and requires a major obesity-related comorbidity alongside the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements; the surgeon will assess the person’s medical history and risks.
+
+4. **BMI 30-35**
+
+> BMI 30–35: may be considered in select cases, often where diabetes is difficult to manage with medication alone; assessed on an individual basis
+
+> BMI below 35: this does not meet the MBS threshold for these items; discuss any non-MBS clinical options with the surgeon, who will assess suitability individually.
+
+Why it matters: The MBS pathway is BMI 40+, or BMI 35+ with a major comorbidity. Nothing below BMI 35 qualifies under MBS, and all BMI wording must remain assessment-only.
+
+### G3 — concrete cut-list, FAQ answers and link-ready recommendations
+
+The cut-list is:
+
+- **Delete the extraction instruction:** “Closing comparison note (below paragraphs): Orient the reader to what they will find next — a plain overview of the four main surgical options so they can walk into their consultation with a sense of what the conversation will cover.” Replace it with: “Below is a plain overview of the main surgical and non-surgical pathways, with links to more detailed procedure pages if you want to compare options before your consultation.”
+- **Replace the process block:** cut the block beginning “Initial consultation and assessment Here you’ll meet your surgeon...” and ending “...bloodwork done to protect your nutrition and results long-term.” Replace it with six concise steps: GP referral/enquiry; surgeon consultation; wider-team assessment; pre-surgery preparation; procedure day; long-term aftercare.
+- **Replace the procedure block:** cut the block beginning “Gastric sleeve (sleeve gastrectomy): our most common procedure...” and ending “Medical weight management: non-surgical weight loss treatment pathways including GLP-1 medications...” Replace it with one neutral sentence and one link for each of sleeve, Roux-en-Y bypass, mini bypass, band, revision and medical weight management. Use the G1 replacements above, then link to `/gastric-sleeve-perth`, `/gastric-bypass-perth`, `/mini-gastric-bypass`, `/gastric-band`, `/revision-bariatric-surgery` and `/non-surgical-weight-loss`.
+- **Merge the risk bullets:** cut the bullets beginning “General surgical risks include bleeding, infection...” and ending “Revision surgery may be required in some cases...” and replace them with three compact groups: general and procedure-specific surgical risks; nutritional and eating changes; weight regain, ongoing support and possible revision. Keep the risk list frank and measured.
+- **Replace the seven-answer FAQ block:** cut the block beginning “FAQs (7 questions)” and ending “your surgeon will assess your history carefully before making any recommendation.” Replace it with the FAQ block below; this is the largest word-count cut and adds the missing buyer answers.
+
+The link-ready replacement for the opening procedure/service sentence is:
+
+> Obesity Surgery WA offers [gastric sleeve surgery](/gastric-sleeve-perth), [gastric bypass surgery](/gastric-bypass-perth), [mini gastric bypass](/mini-gastric-bypass), [gastric band surgery](/gastric-band), [revision bariatric surgery](/revision-bariatric-surgery) and [non-surgical weight management](/non-surgical-weight-loss). See [eligibility](/am-i-eligible), [Medicare and private health cover](/medicare-private-health-cover), [GP referral](/gp-referral), [surgeons](/surgeons), [FAQs](/faqs) and [book a consultation](/book-consultation).
+
+#### Paste-ready FAQ replacement
+
+Replace the existing FAQ heading and answers with:
+
+> ### FAQs
+>
+> **1. How much does bariatric surgery cost in Perth?**
+>
+> There is no single Perth price. Total costs vary by procedure, hospital, surgeon, anaesthetist, tests, private-health cover, excess and waiting periods. Any range or estimate should be discussed at consultation with an itemised breakdown; ask your insurer which benefits and gaps apply before booking.
+>
+> **2. Is bariatric surgery covered by Medicare or private health insurance?**
+>
+> Medicare benefits may apply when the relevant MBS item, clinical and referral requirements are met, but eligibility is assessed individually. Verify the planned item number: 31575 is sleeve gastrectomy, 31572 is Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, and 31581 is BPD/duodenal switch. Private-health benefits depend on the fund, policy, waiting periods and exclusions.
+>
+> **3. How do I know which bariatric procedure is right for me?**
+>
+> The choice depends on your medical history, eating patterns, health conditions, goals and willingness to follow the required aftercare. Your surgeon should explain likely benefits, limits, risks and monitoring needs for each option; no procedure or outcome is guaranteed.
+>
+> **4. What is the difference between a gastric sleeve and a gastric bypass?**
+>
+> A sleeve removes part of the stomach without rerouting the small intestine. A Roux-en-Y bypass creates a smaller stomach pouch and reroutes part of the small intestine. Each has different risks, nutritional monitoring and follow-up needs, which your surgeon can explain for your circumstances.
+>
+> **5. Do I have to have surgery, or are there non-surgical options?**
+>
+> Surgery is not the only pathway. Some people may discuss medical weight management, dietitian-led support or a staged approach before considering surgery. The clinical team should assess which options are appropriate and explain their limits, follow-up requirements and risks.
+>
+> **6. What is the 30/30 eating rule after bariatric surgery?**
+>
+> Many bariatric programs use a 30/30 approach: take about 30 minutes to eat and separate drinks from meals by about 30 minutes. It is a general postoperative guide, not a universal rule; your surgeon or dietitian will tailor advice to your procedure and stage of recovery.
+>
+> **7. Who may not be eligible for bariatric surgery?**
+>
+> No online checklist can decide suitability. For MBS items, a BMI below 35 does not meet the threshold; a procedure may also be deferred if assessment identifies unacceptable surgical or anaesthetic risk, unresolved medical issues or difficulty completing required follow-up. Your surgeon makes an individual assessment.
+>
+> **8. What does aftercare involve, and what can happen over 10 years?**
+>
+> Aftercare can include surgeon reviews, blood tests, dietitian support, supplements where prescribed and help adapting eating. Over 10 years, experiences vary: some people maintain changes, some regain weight, develop nutritional issues or need further treatment. No single 10-year result can be promised, so ongoing follow-up remains important.
+>
+> **9. Where will my surgery and appointments take place?**
+>
+> Appointments take place at the confirmed OSWA clinic locations, and surgery takes place at the hospital selected with your surgeon. Hospital names and affiliations must be verified before publication; the team should confirm the location, admission process and follow-up arrangements during planning.
+>
+> **10. I have had weight loss surgery before — can it be revised?**
+>
+> Revision surgery may be discussed after a previous procedure when there are complications, a need for conversion or another clinical reason. It can involve different risks and follow-up from a first procedure, so the surgeon needs to review your operative history and current health before advising.
+>
+> **11. How should I choose a bariatric surgeon in Perth?**
+>
+> Ask about specialist registration or FRACS status, relevant bariatric experience, verified hospital access, multidisciplinary aftercare and how risks, costs and complications are explained. Ask who provides follow-up and what happens if concerns arise. Avoid claims that any individual surgeon or clinic is “the best.”
+
